<p class="lede">The current release is Bayan 0.4.1, a prerelease. Download the archive for your machine, or build the compiler from source with only a C compiler.</p>

<h2 id="requirements">What you need</h2>
<ul>
<li>A machine running macOS, Linux or Windows, on x64 or arm64.</li>
<li>A C compiler, which your programs also need: Bayan turns your program into C and hands it over. Install <a href="https://ziglang.org/download/">zig</a> on your <code>PATH</code>; <code>zig cc</code> also builds for other targets. The compiler uses <code>BAYAN_CC</code> if set, else <code>zig cc</code>, else <code>cc</code>, <code>clang</code> or <code>gcc</code> from <code>PATH</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="download">Download a release</h2>
<p>Download the archive for your machine from the <a href="https://github.com/ikhbat-org/bayan/releases">releases page</a>, with <code>SHA256SUMS</code> beside it. Names follow <code>bayan-&lt;version&gt;-&lt;os&gt;-&lt;arch&gt;.tar.gz</code> (<code>.zip</code> on Windows), and each holds a <code>bayan-&lt;version&gt;-&lt;os&gt;-&lt;arch&gt;/</code> folder with <code>bayan</code> and the language server <code>bayan-lsp</code> (<code>bayan.exe</code> and <code>bayan-lsp.exe</code> on Windows).</p>
<p>Check it against <code>SHA256SUMS</code>, unpack, and put <code>bayan</code> on your <code>PATH</code>:</p>
<pre><code>grep macos-arm64.tar.gz SHA256SUMS | shasum -a 256 -c
tar -xzf bayan-0.4.1-macos-arm64.tar.gz
cp bayan-0.4.1-macos-arm64/bayan ~/.local/bin/</code></pre>
<p>On Linux, <code>sha256sum -c</code> replaces <code>shasum -a 256 -c</code>; on Windows, unzip and put <code>bayan.exe</code> on your <code>PATH</code>:</p>
<pre><code>Expand-Archive bayan-0.4.1-windows-x64.zip -DestinationPath .
$env:Path += ";$PWD\bayan-0.4.1-windows-x64"</code></pre>

<h2 id="check">Check the install</h2>
<pre><code>bayan --version
bayan doctor</code></pre>
<p><code>--version</code> prints <code>bayan 0.4.1 (selfhost)</code>. <code>doctor</code> checks the C compiler, zig, git and the build cache and gives fixes; on a working machine every line starts with a diamond and names a path. Without a C compiler, <code>bayan run</code> and <code>bayan build</code> exit 4.</p>
<p>Run the first program:</p>
<example name="hello"/>
<pre><code>bayan run hello.byn</code></pre>
<p>Next: <a href="tutorial.html">Your first Bayan program</a>.</p>

<h2 id="build">Build the compiler from source</h2>
<p>The compiler is written in Bayan and compiles itself; the repository also keeps it as one C file, <code>bootstrap/bayan.c</code>, so a C11 compiler is enough. From a clone:</p>
<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/ikhbat-org/bayan
cd bayan
cc -std=c11 -O2 -DBYN_NDEBUG -fwrapv -ffp-contract=off bootstrap/bayan.c bootstrap/bayan-rt.c -lm
./a.out --version</code></pre>
<p>Warnings about the generated C do not stop the build. The result is <code>a.out</code>; copy it onto your <code>PATH</code> as <code>bayan</code>. To rebuild from the Bayan source in <code>src/</code> with that compiler:</p>
<pre><code>bayan build --mode safe --package src -o tmp/bayan-selfhost src/driver</code></pre>

<h2 id="dist">Release binaries under dist/</h2>
<p><code>scripts/dist.sh</code> builds the compiler from <code>src/</code>, starting from <code>bootstrap/bayan.c</code>, into <code>dist/</code>: one directory per target and version, plus a per-target <code>latest</code> that every build replaces:</p>
<pre><code>scripts/dist.sh                 # this machine
scripts/dist.sh --rid osx-x64   # another target, cross-built through zig
dist/macos-arm64/latest/bayan --version</code></pre>
<p>Targets are <code>linux-x64</code>, <code>linux-arm64</code>, and the same for <code>macos</code> and <code>windows</code>. Each version directory holds <code>bayan</code>, <code>bayan-lsp</code> (with <code>.exe</code> on Windows) and <code>BUILD-INFO</code> with the version, target, commit and build time; a binary built on another machine can be imported with <code>--from</code>.</p>
<p><code>bayan dist</code> is different: it builds release binaries of <em>your</em> program into <code>dist/&lt;version&gt;/</code>.</p>

<h2 id="commands">The commands</h2>
<p>Output of <code>bayan --help</code>, as the compiler prints it:</p>
<pre><code>Usage: bayan &lt;command&gt; [options]

  new              Create a project in a new directory
  init             Add a project's missing files to a directory

  build            Compile a program to an executable
  run              Compile and run a program
  test             Compile and run a program's tests
  check            Report a program's diagnostics
  &lt;script&gt;.byn     Run a script, so `#!/usr/bin/env bayan` works

  format           Rewrite `.byn` files into their canonical form
  fix              Apply every style lint's fix
  audit            List every `because` bypass and `unsafe` or `extern`
  explain          Explain a diagnostic, e.g. `bayan explain BYN0003`
  doctor           Check the C compiler, zig, git and the build cache
  clean            Remove out/, and dist/ or the build cache
  dist             Build release binaries into dist/&lt;version&gt;/

  lex              Print a file's token stream
  parse            Check a file's syntax or print its syntax tree
  types            Print each function's parameter and local types
  features         Print the syntax, builtins and diagnostics a build used

General options:
  -h, --help       Print this help, or after a command that command's help
  -v, --version    Print the version line, `bayan &lt;version&gt;`
Run 'bayan help &lt;command&gt;' for a command's options.</code></pre>
